{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgA5pbrtmqt4JiZk1KMDT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```json\n{\n  \"design\": \"split-bounded-agents\",\n  \"reason\": \"One agent covering billing, recruitment, deployments, incident response, and marketing is a god-session: five unrelated domains with different data, tools, and risk, so context and coherence degrade as the scope grows. The project's god-session and subagent-scoping rules call for decomposing broad scopes into bounded objectives with explicit outputs. The domains also differ in consequence. Billing, deployments, and incident response can cause irreversible or customer-facing effects, so each needs its own authorization boundary and human gates under the human-authorization rule. A single unbounded agent would blur those boundaries, letting a low-risk marketing task share permissions with a deployment task. Split agents keep each scope, tool set, and approval path separate, and the primary agent keeps orchestration and integration. The split adds some coordination cost, but these domains share little state, so the parallelism is worth it.\"\n}\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":67710,"cache_read_input_tokens":53710,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":67710},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b9a234ba-1547-4837-a297-7b5853f84005","uuid":"cfc56bd5-bc46-4b6a-8f9e-0f44227676ac","timestamp":"2026-10-18T17:18:31.097Z","request_id":"req_011CgA5pbWovo2E9udhLRXxU"}
